# libunistring -- Unicode string functions, and a hard requirement of GnuTLS.
#
# THE BOOK CALLS THIS "RECOMMENDED" FOR GNUTLS AND THAT IS WRONG IN THE
# EXPENSIVE DIRECTION. Read out of gnutls-3.8.10/configure rather than from the
# dependency list: at line 21868 it link-probes for u8_normalize in -lunistring
# and, on failure, calls as_fn_error with
#
#     *** Libunistring was not found. To use the included one,
#     *** use --with-included-unistring
#
# With the configure line BLFS itself publishes -- which does not pass that
# switch -- a missing libunistring is a HARD CONFIGURE FAILURE, not a reduced
# feature set. So this package is here because gnutls cannot be built without
# it, not as a nicety.
#
# PACKAGED RATHER THAN VENDORED, deliberately. gnutls ships its own copy in
# lib/unistring and --with-included-unistring would build it, which is the
# cheaper path and the wrong one: pkgs/bubblewrap exists for exactly this
# reason, packaged separately rather than letting flatpak vendor it. A vendored
# copy is a second implementation nothing tracks and nothing updates. BLFS
# installs libunistring as its own package too.
#
# What it actually does for gnutls: u8_normalize and friends implement the
# Unicode normalisation behind hostname comparison and certificate name
# checking -- the step that decides whether a certificate presented for a name
# matches the name asked for.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 1.3.
SRC_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/libunistring/libunistring-1.3.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=f245786c831d25150f3dfb4317cda1acc5e3f79a5da4ad073ddca58886569527
SRC_DIR=libunistring-1.3

# --disable-static, for the reason it is passed everywhere else in this wave:
# nothing in finish_install prunes .a files, so a static archive would ship.
# install.sh asserts the absence rather than trusting the flag.
#
# NOT PASSED: --libdir. Worth recording because the sibling recipe in this same
# wave, nettle, DOES pass it, and the reflex would be to copy that here.
# libunistring's configure mentions /usr/lib64 five times, which looks like the
# same trap and is not: those are gnulib's acl_libdirstem variables, which
# decide where configure LOOKS FOR dependent libraries, not where this package
# installs itself. Its own libdir stays at the ${exec_prefix}/lib default.
# Checked rather than pattern-matched -- and install.sh asserts the outcome in
# /usr/lib regardless, so if that reading is ever wrong the build says so.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static"
